{"meta": {"course": "ai-ethics", "chapter": "02", "section": "01", "chapter_title": "Bias and Fairness", "title": "Module 1: Algorithmic Bias and Where It Comes From", "description": "Disentangle the six sources of algorithmic bias (historical, representation, measurement, aggregation, evaluation, deployment feedback) and match each to its appropriate intervention.", "learning_objectives": ["Name and distinguish six sources of algorithmic bias.", "Diagnose which bias sources are present in a real-world AI failure case.", "Match each bias source to an appropriate engineering or non-engineering intervention.", "Recognize when post-processing 'debiasing' cannot fix the underlying problem."], "concept_ids": ["algorithmic_bias", "historical_bias", "representation_bias", "measurement_bias", "aggregation_bias", "evaluation_bias", "deployment_feedback_bias"], "prereq_section_ids": ["ai-ethics/01/02"], "practice_ids": ["bias_face_recog_source", "bias_proxy_problem", "bias_predictive_policing", "bias_debiasing_limit"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1}, "content": "# Module 1: Algorithmic Bias and Where It Comes From\n\n> **\"Bias deep in the model is a property of the data, the problem framing, and the deployment, not just the algorithm.\"**  \n> - paraphrasing Barocas, Hardt, and Narayanan, *Fairness and Machine Learning*\n\n---\n\n## Opening: One Word, Six Failures\n\nIn October 2019, Ziad Obermeyer and colleagues published a finding in *Science* about a commercial risk-prediction algorithm used to manage care for roughly 200 million people in the United States each year. The algorithm was meant to flag the sickest patients for extra support. It worked exactly as designed, and it still produced a racial disparity: at any given risk score, Black patients were substantially sicker than white patients with the same score. The reason was subtle. The vendor had trained the model to predict future **healthcare cost**, reasoning that costly patients are sick patients. But because less money had historically been spent on Black patients with the same conditions, cost was a distorted stand-in for need. Correcting the flaw would have raised the share of Black patients flagged for extra care from 17.7% to 46.5%.\n\nNobody in this story wrote a line of racist code. There was no malicious actor and no obvious bug. The harm came from a reasonable-sounding modeling choice colliding with an unequal world.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** When a deployed AI system performs systematically worse for one group than another, journalists, regulators, and engineers all call it \"bias.\" The word is not wrong, but it is too compressed to be diagnostic. Bias can enter from at least six different places, each with a different fix and a different responsible party. Calling all of them \"bias\" produces one-size-fits-all \"debiasing\" that often targets the wrong layer and leaves the harm intact.\n\nThis module disentangles the standard taxonomy used by Suresh and Guttag (2021), by Barocas, Hardt, and Narayanan in *Fairness and Machine Learning*, and by NIST's AI Risk Management Framework. Once you can name the *kind* of bias a system has, you can pick the right intervention.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Name and distinguish** the six sources of algorithmic bias and where in the pipeline each one enters.\n2. **Diagnose** which bias sources are present in a real-world AI failure case using a structured workflow.\n3. **Match** each bias source to an appropriate engineering or non-engineering intervention.\n4. **Recognize** when post-processing \"debiasing\" cannot, even in principle, fix the underlying problem.\n\n---\n\n## Locating Bias in the ML Pipeline\n\nA useful mental model: every machine learning system is a pipeline, and each of the six bias sources attaches to a specific stage. Naming the stage tells you who can act and what kind of action is even possible.\n\n| Pipeline Stage | Bias Source That Enters Here | Who Can Fix It |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| The world generating the data | Historical | Institution, policy, product owner |\n| Sampling the data | Representation | Data engineering, collection |\n| Choosing what to record/label | Measurement | Domain experts, target redesign |\n| Choosing one model for all | Aggregation | ML architecture |\n| Choosing the test set | Evaluation | ML evaluation, QA |\n| Acting on predictions over time | Deployment / feedback | System design, governance |\n\n> **Key Insight:** \"Bias\" is not located in the algorithm alone. Four of the six sources enter before a single weight is learned, and one enters only after deployment. This is why a brilliant model on a flawed problem framing is still a biased system.\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 1: Historical Bias\n\nThe data may accurately reflect a world that is already unjust. A hiring model trained on resumes from a decade in which mostly men were hired will accurately predict that the company hires men. The model is not lying; the world it was trained on was discriminatory, and the model faithfully reproduced that pattern.\n\n**Sign:** the model performs as designed, but its outputs entrench an existing social inequity.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** more data of the same kind; \"debiasing\" the predictions while leaving the target intact.\n\n**Right fixes:** change the target variable away from the contaminated proxy; redesign the loss to penalize harms; ask whether the model should be deployed at all.\n\nThe Amazon hiring model that downranked the word \"women's\" from resumes (e.g., \"women's chess club captain\") is the canonical example. The data was historically accurate. The accurate prediction was the harm.\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 2: Representation (Sampling) Bias\n\nThe training distribution does not match the deployment distribution. A facial-recognition model trained mostly on light-skinned faces performs worse on dark-skinned faces. A speech model trained on US-accented English performs worse on second-language English. A medical imaging model trained at three urban hospitals performs worse on the patient population of a fourth rural hospital.\n\n**Sign:** error rates differ systematically across subgroups present at deployment time.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** add more data overall.\n\n**Right fixes:** stratified resampling that balances subgroup representation; data collection from underrepresented groups; subgroup-disaggregated evaluation reports (Buolamwini and Gebru's *Gender Shades* methodology).\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 3: Measurement Bias\n\nTwo different groups have the same underlying state but the *measurement* of that state differs across them. Healthcare cost-prediction models that used **annual healthcare expenditure** as a proxy for **healthcare need** systematically underestimated the need of Black patients, because the same need was historically met with less spending. Obermeyer et al. (2019, *Science*) traced this in a widely deployed risk algorithm.\n\n**Sign:** the proxy is correlated with what you want to predict, but the correlation differs across groups.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** trust the proxy; add more proxies of the same family.\n\n**Right fixes:** define what you actually want to measure; check the proxy against direct measurements in subgroups; sometimes change the target.\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 4: Aggregation Bias\n\nA single model is used across subpopulations that the model does not handle equally well. The \"one model for everyone\" assumption fails when groups have different optimal decision boundaries. A diabetes risk model trained on a mixed population may underperform on every subgroup compared to per-subgroup models.\n\n**Sign:** the model has lower accuracy on every subgroup than a per-subgroup model would.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** ignore subgroup structure; report only aggregate accuracy.\n\n**Right fixes:** subgroup-specific models, mixture-of-experts architectures, or hierarchical Bayesian models that share strength across groups while respecting their differences.\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 5: Evaluation Bias\n\nThe benchmark used to validate the model is not representative of the deployment population. A model that scores well on ImageNet is not validated for medical imagery. A reading-level model that scores well on standardized test passages is not validated on clinical notes. The evaluation procedure itself encodes assumptions.\n\n**Sign:** lab metrics look great, deployment performance is materially worse.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** more eyes on the same benchmark.\n\n**Right fixes:** representative test sets, subgroup-disaggregated metrics, deployment-context evaluation, ongoing monitoring.\n\n---\n\n## Bias Source 6: Deployment (Feedback Loop) Bias\n\nThe act of deploying the model changes the world it operates in, which then feeds back into the next training round. Predictive policing systems that send more patrols to neighborhoods predicted to be high-crime then record more crime there, which trains the next model to predict more crime there. The feedback loop manufactures the data it needs to confirm itself.\n\n**Sign:** small initial differences in predictions amplify over time without corresponding changes in the underlying world.\n\n**Wrong fixes:** retrain on more data (that is the trap).\n\n**Right fixes:** counterfactual evaluation, randomized exploration to break the loop, deployment-time fairness constraints, periodic re-grounding against external truth.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"bias-source-taxonomy\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Diagnostic Workflow\n\nWhen a system is reported as \"biased,\" walk the six sources in order:\n\n1. Is the **training data** itself the record of an unjust world? (Historical)\n2. Does the **distribution** of the training data fail to represent deployment subgroups? (Representation)\n3. Is the **proxy** for what you want to measure unequal across groups? (Measurement)\n4. Are subgroups being **lumped** under one model that fits none of them well? (Aggregation)\n5. Is the **benchmark** used to validate the model unrepresentative? (Evaluation)\n6. Is the system's own deployment **manufacturing** the data that confirms it? (Deployment / feedback)\n\nMost real cases involve more than one. A complete diagnosis names which sources are present, in what proportion, and which can actually be fixed at the engineering layer versus which require a non-engineering response (changing the target, declining to deploy, changing the institution).\n\n<inline-check question=\"A predictive-policing model sends more patrols to a neighborhood, those patrols record more incidents, and next year's model is trained on that richer record and predicts even more crime there. Which diagnostic question catches this?\" explanation=\"This is the signature of deployment / feedback bias: the system's own actions manufacture the data that confirms its predictions, so disparities amplify over time without any matching change in the underlying world. Retraining on the new data is the trap, not the fix.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Is the proxy for what you want to measure unequal across groups? (Measurement)\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Is the system&#39;s own deployment manufacturing the data that confirms it? (Deployment / feedback)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Does the distribution of the training data fail to represent deployment subgroups? (Representation)\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nOnce you can name the source, the next skill is ordering the diagnosis itself.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"State the observed harm precisely (which group, which decision)\",\"Is the training data the record of an unjust world? (Historical)\",\"Does the sample under-represent deployment subgroups? (Representation)\",\"Is the proxy mismeasured across groups? (Measurement)\",\"Are subgroups lumped under one ill-fitting model? (Aggregation)\",\"Is the validation benchmark unrepresentative? (Evaluation)\",\"Is deployment manufacturing its own confirming data? (Feedback)\",\"Separate engineering-layer fixes from non-engineering responses\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Case Analysis: The Care-Management Algorithm\n\nLet us run the full diagnostic on the Obermeyer case from the opening, then turn the diagnosis into a decision. This is the move that separates a slogan (\"the algorithm was biased\") from an analysis a team can act on.\n\n### Step 1: State the claim precisely\n\nThe observable harm: among patients assigned the same risk score, Black patients had more active chronic conditions than white patients. So at the threshold used to enroll patients in extra care, qualified Black patients were being passed over. The disparity was in **who got help**, not in raw predictive accuracy of the cost target.\n\n### Step 2: Walk the six sources\n\n| Source | Present? | Evidence in this case |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Historical | Partly | Less was historically spent on Black patients; that pattern is real and unjust. |\n| Representation | No strong sign | The training population resembled the deployment population; the failure was not a sampling gap. |\n| **Measurement** | **Primary** | Cost was used as a proxy for need; the proxy-to-need relationship differs by group. |\n| Aggregation | Minor | One model served all groups, but per-group models would not fix a mismeasured target. |\n| Evaluation | Secondary | The model was validated on cost prediction, not on need-disaggregated subgroup outcomes. |\n| Deployment feedback | Risk | Under-served patients incur lower future cost, which would re-confirm the proxy next round. |\n\nThe dominant source is **measurement bias**: a proxy (cost) that correlates with the goal (need) but with a group-dependent slope. Evaluation bias hid it (nobody tested need by subgroup), and a feedback loop would have entrenched it.\n\n### Step 3: Identify stakeholders and what each one bears\n\n| Stakeholder | What they want | What the biased system does to them |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Under-served patients | Care matched to need | Systematically under-enrolled despite higher illness |\n| Health system | Cost control, good outcomes | Misallocates scarce care-management slots |\n| Algorithm vendor | A sellable, accurate product | Optimized the wrong target; reputational and legal exposure |\n| Regulator | Equitable, accountable care | Must detect a harm invisible in aggregate accuracy |\n\n### Step 4: Weigh the interventions\n\n| Candidate fix | Addresses the real source? | Tradeoff |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Add more training data | No | Same proxy, more of it; harm unchanged |\n| Post-process scores by race | No (and legally fraught) | Reshuffles error; target still mismeasured |\n| **Change the target** to a direct need measure (e.g., number of active chronic conditions) | **Yes** | Needs new labels and domain agreement; this is what the researchers showed reduced the disparity by ~84% |\n| Subgroup-disaggregated evaluation | Detects, does not cure | Cheap monitoring; must be paired with a target fix |\n| Decline to deploy | Removes harm | Forfeits genuine benefit of triage; rarely the best option here |\n\n### Step 5: Decide\n\nThe defensible decision is to **redefine the prediction target** from cost to a direct measure of health need, and to **add subgroup-disaggregated evaluation** as a standing monitor. Both the engineering layer (re-label, re-train, re-evaluate) and the institutional layer (agree on what \"need\" means) must act. Post-processing alone would have been the seductive wrong answer: cheap, fast, and aimed at the wrong layer.\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** Notice that the highest-leverage fix here, changing the target variable, is not a hyperparameter or a fairness constraint. It is a reframing of the problem. The most consequential fairness decisions are often made before any model is trained.\n\nWork the same case again, this time predicting the diagnosis before each reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Re-derive the care-management diagnosis\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The model predicts future healthcare COST and uses it to flag who needs extra care. At a fixed score, Black patients turn out sicker. The cost label tracks need, but historically less was spent on equally-sick Black patients. Which single source is the PRIMARY driver?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Representation bias (the sample missed a subgroup)\",\"Measurement bias (the proxy maps to the goal differently by group)\",\"Aggregation bias (one model fit no subgroup)\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Measurement is primary: cost is a proxy for need whose slope-to-need differs by group, so equal scores hide unequal need.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Why did nobody catch it during validation?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The benchmark measured cost-prediction accuracy, never need disaggregated by subgroup\",\"The training set was too small\",\"The model was overfit\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Evaluation bias hid it: the model was validated on the cost target, not on need broken out by group, so the harm was invisible in aggregate accuracy.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which fix actually removes the harm rather than relocating it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Add more cost-labeled training data\",\"Post-process scores by race\",\"Change the target from cost to a direct need measure (active chronic conditions)\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Redefining the target cut the disparity by roughly 84% in the published reanalysis. The cheap post-processing answer leaves a mismeasured target untouched.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Diagnosis complete: Measurement (primary) + Evaluation (concealing) + a latent feedback risk. The decisive lever lived before training, in the choice of what to predict.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## What \"Debiasing\" Cannot Fix\n\nA widely circulated belief is that \"post-processing\" the model's predictions can eliminate bias. It cannot in general. Post-processing can change which group bears which kind of error, but it cannot create information the model never had, and it cannot rewrite the target variable.\n\nIf the bias is **historical**, post-processing reshuffles the harm. If the bias is **measurement**, post-processing applies a transform to a measurement that was never measuring the right thing. If the bias is **deployment feedback**, post-processing each output does not break the loop.\n\nThe only intervention that is robust across all six sources is **examining whether the system should be built or deployed at all**, the question Bender, Gebru, McMillan-Major, and Mitchell raise in their \"stochastic parrots\" critique: do we know what this model is reproducing, and is that worth reproducing?\n\nYou are the ML lead. The audit just surfaced the measurement bias. Now you must decide under real constraints.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The care-management dilemma: ship, patch, or pull?\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The audit confirms cost is a group-dependent proxy for need. Product wants to keep the model live during the holiday enrollment window. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Ship a quick post-processing layer that nudges scores up for the under-served group\",\"next\":\"patch\",\"feedback\":\"Fast and politically easy, but you are reshaping error on top of a target that never measured need.\"},{\"label\":\"Re-label and retrain on a direct need measure (active chronic conditions) before re-enrolling\",\"next\":\"relabel\",\"feedback\":\"Attacks the real source, but takes weeks and needs clinical sign-off on what need means.\"},{\"label\":\"Add more cost-labeled training data to stabilize the model\",\"next\":\"moredata\",\"feedback\":\"More of the same proxy. The disparity is structural, not a sample-size problem.\"}]},\"patch\":{\"prompt\":\"Six months later, enrolled-but-low-cost patients show low future spend, and the next training round re-confirms the old proxy. What happened?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A feedback loop entrenched the mismeasured target\",\"next\":\"loop\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Post-processing never broke the cost target, so deployment manufactured confirming data.\"},{\"label\":\"The patch simply needs retuning\",\"next\":\"patch\",\"feedback\":\"Retuning a transform on a wrong target cannot create the missing information. You are back where you started.\"}]},\"loop\":{\"prompt\":\"You now propose the target change you skipped. Final move?\",\"feedback\":\"Right outcome, later and costlier. The cheap patch delayed the only robust fix and let a feedback loop accrue harm. Lesson: post-processing relocates error; it cannot rewrite a mismeasured target.\",\"choices\":[]},\"relabel\":{\"prompt\":\"Clinicians agree on the need definition; the disparity drops about 84%. What standing safeguard do you add so this cannot silently recur?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Subgroup-disaggregated evaluation as a permanent monitor\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Detection plus a corrected target is the durable combination.\"},{\"label\":\"Trust the new model and remove monitoring to cut cost\",\"next\":\"relapse\",\"feedback\":\"Without disaggregated monitoring, the next drift is invisible again. Detection is cheap insurance.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"You shipped a corrected target plus standing subgroup monitoring.\",\"feedback\":\"Best path: you fixed the layer where the bias actually lived (the target) and instrumented the layer that hid it (evaluation). Both the engineering and institutional layers acted.\",\"choices\":[]},\"relapse\":{\"prompt\":\"Drift returns unseen.\",\"feedback\":\"A good fix without monitoring decays. Measurement and evaluation bias must be addressed together.\",\"choices\":[]},\"moredata\":{\"prompt\":\"Accuracy on the cost target improves; the need disparity is unchanged. Next?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Recognize the source is the target, not the data volume, and switch to re-labeling\",\"next\":\"relabel\",\"feedback\":\"Correct pivot. Volume cannot cure a proxy that measures the wrong thing.\"}]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why post-processing the predictions cannot fix measurement or historical bias, and name the one intervention that is robust across all six sources.\" answer=\"Post-processing only redistributes error among groups; it cannot create information the model never captured, nor can it rewrite a target variable that was measuring the wrong quantity (measurement bias) or a label that faithfully records an unjust world (historical bias). The transform sits downstream of the contaminated target, so the contamination flows through unchanged. The only intervention robust across all six sources is stepping back to ask whether the system should be built or deployed at all, and what exactly it would be reproducing.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limitations\n\nA taxonomy is a tool, not the territory. Use it with these cautions in mind.\n\n- **The categories overlap.** Measurement and historical bias in particular shade into each other; the Obermeyer case can be told as either, depending on whether you emphasize the unequal spending (historical) or the proxy choice (measurement). The point of the taxonomy is not crisp classification, it is to surface *every* contributing layer so none is missed.\n- **Naming the source does not resolve the fairness definition.** Even after a clean diagnosis, \"fair\" can mean equal error rates, equal false-positive rates, calibration within groups, or individual consistency. Kleinberg, Mullainathan, and Raghavan (2016) proved that several of these cannot all hold at once except in degenerate cases. The taxonomy tells you *where* the bias enters; it does not tell you *which* fairness criterion to optimize.\n- **Some \"fixes\" trade one group's harm for another's.** Post-processing and threshold adjustment redistribute error rather than removing it. A fix that improves one subgroup's false-negative rate may worsen another's false-positive rate. There is rarely a free lunch.\n- **Group-blind is not bias-free.** Removing a protected attribute from the features does not remove the bias, because correlated proxies (ZIP code, name, purchase history) reconstruct it. Blindness can even make bias harder to audit.\n- **A model can be unbiased and still cause harm.** Even a perfectly calibrated, representative model can be deployed in a context where any automated decision concentrates power or removes recourse. The taxonomy diagnoses statistical bias; it is silent on legitimacy.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Algorithmic Bias Sources\" context=\"A school district deploys an AI grading model on student essays. After a year, parents in some neighborhoods report that the model gives their children systematically lower scores than human graders did. Walk the six bias-source diagnostic to identify which sources are most likely contributing and what engineering and non-engineering responses would address each.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A facial-recognition model performs significantly worse on dark-skinned faces. Which bias source is most likely the primary contributor, and which intervention is best matched to it?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Aggregation bias; fix it by training one larger global model.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Representation bias; fix it by stratified resampling, targeted data collection from underrepresented groups, and subgroup-disaggregated evaluation (Gender Shades methodology).\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Deployment feedback bias; fix it with post-processing.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Differential subgroup error driven by skewed training distribution is representation bias. The Buolamwini and Gebru Gender Shades work made this diagnostic the canonical reference.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### The Six Sources at a Glance\n\n| Source | One-line signature | Best-matched response |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Historical | Accurate model of an unjust world | Change target; question deployment |\n| Representation | Subgroup error from skewed sampling | Stratified resampling; targeted collection |\n| Measurement | Group-dependent proxy for the real target | Redefine what you measure |\n| Aggregation | One model fits no subgroup well | Subgroup or hierarchical models |\n| Evaluation | Great in lab, worse in the field | Representative, disaggregated test sets |\n| Deployment feedback | Predictions manufacture confirming data | Counterfactual eval; randomized exploration |\n\nBefore the prose takeaways, see how the six sources hang off the single parent concept.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"algorithmic_bias\",\"historical_bias\",\"representation_bias\",\"measurement_bias\",\"aggregation_bias\",\"evaluation_bias\",\"deployment_feedback_bias\"]'></concept-map>\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- \"Bias\" is six different problems with six different owners; diagnose before you intervene.\n- Four of the six sources enter before training; the fix often lives outside the model.\n- Run the diagnostic in order, and expect most real cases to involve more than one source.\n- A stakeholder and tradeoff analysis turns a diagnosis into a defensible decision.\n- Post-processing redistributes error; it cannot create missing information or rewrite a mismeasured target.\n- A statistically unbiased model can still be an illegitimate one; the taxonomy is necessary, not sufficient.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Module 2: Defining Fairness**, we move from *where bias comes from* to *what fairness even means*. We will formalize competing fairness criteria (demographic parity, equalized odds, calibration), work through the impossibility results that prevent satisfying them all at once, and decide how a team can choose a criterion defensibly for a given context.\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "bias_face_recog_source", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "representation_bias", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["representation_bias", "algorithmic_bias"], "stem": "A facial-recognition model trained mostly on light-skinned faces performs worse on dark-skinned faces. Which bias source is the primary contributor?", "options": ["Historical bias (the world has fewer dark-skinned people).", "Representation bias \u2014 the training distribution does not match the deployment distribution across subgroups.", "Aggregation bias \u2014 one model is being used for too many tasks.", "There is no bias; the model is correct on average."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Differential subgroup error driven by skewed training distribution is representation bias. The Gender Shades work (Buolamwini and Gebru) is the canonical example.", "misconception_id": "all_bias_is_historical"}, {"id": "bias_proxy_problem", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "measurement_bias", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["measurement_bias", "algorithmic_bias"], "stem": "A healthcare risk model uses 'annual healthcare expenditure' as a proxy for 'healthcare need' and underestimates the need of one demographic group. What kind of bias is this?", "options": ["Representation bias.", "Measurement bias \u2014 the proxy correlates with the target differently across groups, because the same need was historically met with less spending in some groups.", "Aggregation bias.", "Deployment feedback bias."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "When the same underlying state produces different observed values across groups, the proxy is measurement-biased. Obermeyer et al. (Science 2019) is the canonical case.", "misconception_id": "proxy_is_neutral"}, {"id": "bias_predictive_policing", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "deployment_feedback_bias", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["deployment_feedback_bias", "algorithmic_bias"], "stem": "A predictive-policing system sends more patrols to neighborhoods predicted to be high-crime, which records more crime there, which trains the next model to predict more crime there. What kind of bias is this and what is the right intervention?", "options": ["Historical bias; collect more data.", "Representation bias; resample.", "Deployment / feedback-loop bias. The system's own deployment manufactures the data that confirms it. Right intervention: counterfactual evaluation, randomized exploration to break the loop, periodic re-grounding against external truth.", "No bias at all."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Self-confirming feedback loops are deployment-time bias. Retraining on more of the loop's own output is the trap; breaking the loop is the fix.", "misconception_id": "more_data_always_helps"}, {"id": "bias_debiasing_limit", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "algorithmic_bias", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["algorithmic_bias", "deployment_feedback_bias"], "stem": "Which statement most accurately describes the limits of post-processing 'debiasing' techniques?", "options": ["Post-processing can eliminate any bias regardless of source.", "Post-processing can change which group bears which kind of error but cannot create information the model never had, rewrite the target variable, or break a deployment feedback loop. Some biases require non-engineering responses (target change, declining to deploy).", "Post-processing is the same as data resampling.", "Bias cannot be reduced at all."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Post-processing reshuffles errors but cannot fix bias rooted in target choice, missing information, or feedback loops. The honest answer is that some interventions live outside engineering.", "misconception_id": "debiasing_is_universal"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "8f5a78be98e9ea79cc9e2d52ea9c9396a6183a733876b177f1d1aef13e8ca54d", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "all_bias_is_historical", "pattern": "single_source_collapse", "description": "Treating all algorithmic bias as historical bias and ignoring representation, measurement, aggregation, evaluation, and feedback-loop sources", "trigger": "learner says 'the data is biased because the world is biased' as a complete diagnosis", "feedback": "Historical bias is one of six sources. Different sources demand different interventions. A diagnosis that names only history will misroute the fix.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "proxy_is_neutral", "pattern": "proxy_blindness", "description": "Assuming a proxy variable measures what it is named to measure equally across groups", "trigger": "learner uses a proxy without checking subgroup correlation with the actual target", "feedback": "Measurement bias appears when the same underlying state produces different proxy values across groups. Always check that the proxy's correlation with the target holds in each subgroup, not just overall.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "more_data_always_helps", "pattern": "data_volume_solution", "description": "Believing additional data always reduces bias regardless of bias source", "trigger": "learner proposes 'collect more data' as the universal fix", "feedback": "More data of the same kind does not fix historical, measurement, or feedback-loop bias. It often deepens them. Diagnose the source first; the right intervention may involve changing target, breaking the loop, or declining to deploy.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "debiasing_is_universal", "pattern": "intervention_overreach", "description": "Believing post-processing 'debiasing' can fix any bias source", "trigger": "learner proposes post-hoc score adjustment as the answer to every bias case", "feedback": "Post-processing reshuffles errors. It cannot create missing information, rewrite the target variable, or break a feedback loop. Some biases are not engineering-fixable.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}